'''blast''' (blȧst), ''v.'' [< ME. ''blasten'', blow, breathe hard; trans., blow, as a trumpet; < ''blast'', a blowing: see ''blast'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To blow; puff; breathe hard; pant. [Scotch and Middle English.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Dragouns . . .
That grisely whistleden and ''blasten''
And of her mouthe fyre outcasten.</poem>
{{right|''King Alisaunder'', l. 5348.}}
<poem>To puffen and to ''blaste''.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', House of Fame, l. 1866.}}}}
'''2.''' To smoke tobacco. [Scotch.]—'''3.''' To boast; brag; speak ostentatiously. ''Scott.'' [Scotch.]—'''4.''' To wither; be blighted.
{{smaller block|<poem>''Blasting'' in the bud,
Losing his verdure, even in the prime.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., i. 1.}}}}
